##
    How far a loop counts, and when it decides.

    Every loop opens with 'loop', and the word after it says which kind: 'loop for' counts,
    'loop each' walks a sequence, 'loop while' asks before each turn, and a bare 'loop' asks
    after — or never, if nothing follows the body but 'end loop'. One opener, and one closer
    for all but the asking-after form, which 'until' closes because the word carrying the
    condition is what ends it.

    Most of this is about the difference between the first two, which is the one that
    surprises people. A range loop reads its header on every turn. A 'loop each' reads its
    sequence once. Both are defensible and the language could not have both, so the rule is
    drawn where the line reads: 'until limit' is a question about limit, and 'in items' is a
    sequence to walk.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.Counting();
        Program.ALiveBound();
        Program.ALiveStep();
        Program.WalkingASequence();
        Program.AFreshVariableEachTurn();
        Program.AskingAfterwards();
        Program.NotAskingAtAll();
    end function

    ##
        @summary: Counting, and where a range loop stops.

        @remarks: 'to' includes the bound it names and 'until' stops short of it, which is the
        distinction other languages leave to remembering whether '<' or '<=' was written.
    ##
    function Counting()
        Console.WriteLine("== to includes, until stops short ==");

        Console.Write("  to 5:    ");
        loop for i = 1 to 5
            Console.Write(i);
            Console.Write(" ");
        end loop
        Console.WriteLine();

        Console.Write("  until 5: ");
        loop for i = 1 until 5
            Console.Write(i);
            Console.Write(" ");
        end loop
        Console.WriteLine();
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A bound that moves while the loop runs.

        @remarks: It is read again at the top of every turn, so it asks about the value as it
        stands rather than as it was.

        Here the bound walks down to meet the counter and the loop ends in the middle. One that
        walked the other way would never end it at all — which is allowed, and is the same
        thing 'while true' is.
    ##
    function ALiveBound()
        Console.WriteLine("== a bound that moves ==");

        integer limit = 10;

        Console.Write("  counted: ");
        loop for i = 1 until limit
            Console.Write(i);
            Console.Write(" ");
            limit = limit - 2;
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("  limit ended at " + limit);
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A step that moves too.

        @remarks: It is read at the same moment as the bound, so a turn reads the header once:
        whatever decided this turn runs is what advances to the next.
    ##
    function ALiveStep()
        Console.WriteLine("== a step that grows ==");

        # 'step' is an ordinary name. The keyword is 'stepby', which is longer for exactly this
        # reason: it can appear nowhere else, so lengthening it costs nothing and leaves the
        # plain word for whoever wants it.
        integer step = 1;

        Console.Write("  counted: ");
        loop for i = 0 until 20 stepby step
            Console.Write(i);
            Console.Write(" ");
            step = step * 2;
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine();

        # A negative step counts down, and 'until' still stops short of its bound.
        Console.Write("  down:    ");
        loop for i = 5 until 0 stepby -1
            Console.Write(i);
            Console.Write(" ");
        end loop
        Console.WriteLine();
    end function

    ##
        @summary: Walking a sequence, whose length is read once.

        @remarks: A 'loop each' names a sequence rather than a bound, so its length is read when
        the loop begins. Changing that sequence inside its own loop is refused rather than left
        to mean something subtle — see samples/negatives/compile/looping.pc. Building a second
        set is the way to say it.
    ##
    function WalkingASequence()
        Console.WriteLine("== walking a sequence ==");

        integer[] amounts = {3, 8, 2, 9};
        integer[] doubled = {};

        loop each amount in amounts
            doubled.Insert(amount * 2);
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine("  original: " + amounts.Join(", "));
        Console.WriteLine("  doubled:  " + doubled.Join(", "));
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A counter that is fresh on every turn.

        @remarks: It belongs to the loop, cannot be assigned to inside the body, and each turn
        gets its own — so a function made during a turn remembers that turn's value rather than
        whatever the counter finished at.

        This is the trap that catches people in languages where one variable is shared and
        every function made in the loop reports the last number.
    ##
    function AFreshVariableEachTurn()
        Console.WriteLine("== a fresh counter every turn ==");

        integer delegate()[] remembered = {};

        loop for i = 1 to 3
            remembered.Insert(() yield i);
        end loop

        Console.Write("  remembered: ");
        loop each made in remembered
            Console.Write(made());
            Console.Write(" ");
        end loop
        Console.WriteLine();
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A loop that asks after the turn rather than before it.

        @remarks: A 'loop' with no word after it runs its body and then asks 'until'. So the
        body always happens at least once, however the answer comes out — which is the whole
        reason to reach for this one rather than 'loop while'.

        The condition is written at the bottom because that is where it is tested. Written at
        the top it would look exactly like a 'loop while' and behave differently, and nothing
        in the line would say so.

        It is also the only loop 'end' does not close: 'until' carries the condition, so it is
        already saying the loop is over.
    ##
    function AskingAfterwards()
        Console.WriteLine("== asking after the turn ==");

        integer countdown = 3;

        Console.Write("  counted down: ");
        loop
            Console.Write(countdown + " ");
            countdown = countdown - 1;
        until countdown == 0

        Console.WriteLine();

        # Already past its condition, and the body still runs — which a 'loop while' would not.
        integer past = 100;

        Console.Write("  already past: ");
        loop
            Console.Write(past);
        until past > 0

        Console.WriteLine();
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A loop with no condition anywhere.

        @remarks: Written when the reason to stop is not a question that can be asked at the
        top or the bottom — it is something that happens partway through. Saying so plainly
        beats writing a condition that is always true and hoping the reader sees what it was
        standing in for.

        Something in it still has to end it: a 'break', a 'yield', or a 'throw'. A loop with
        none of the three is PC0406, an opinion rather than an error, because a program that
        really means to run until it is stopped from outside is one somebody may write.

        What it buys beyond reading well: a loop left only by 'yield' has no way of falling out
        the bottom, so a function may end in one and still satisfy the rule that every path
        yields a value.
    ##
    function NotAskingAtAll()
        Console.WriteLine("== no condition at all ==");

        integer[] readings = {4, 9, 0, 7};
        integer at = 0;

        Console.Write("  up to the first zero: ");

        loop
            if at >= readings.Count
                break;
            end if

            if readings[at] == 0
                break;
            end if

            Console.Write(readings[at] + " ");
            at = at + 1;
        end loop

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("  stopped at index " + at);
    end function

end model
